<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<configuration>
  <packageSources>
    <!-- Restore exclusively from nuget.org so builds are reproducible regardless of any
         machine-wide sources a developer may have configured. -->
    <clear />
    <add key="nuget.org" value="https://api.nuget.org/v3/index.json" protocolVersion="3" />
    <!--
      The package-parity gate's local feed, MIRRORED from test/OptunaSharp.Tests.PackageParity/NuGet.config
      so IDE restores can find it.

      WHY IT MUST LIVE HERE TOO
        Rider ("RestoreEngine = Embedded") and Visual Studio restore with their own NuGet engine, which
        reads settings starting at the SOLUTION folder (this file, then the user and machine configs) and
        never a project folder's NuGet.config. So in an IDE the parity project's own config, the only file
        that adds feed/, is ignored, and its 19 OptunaSharp PackageReferences fail with
        "NU1101: Unable to find package OptunaSharp... in source(s): ...library-packs, nuget.org".
        The CLI (dotnet restore / build / test) starts the walk in each project's folder instead, so on the
        CLI the parity project's own NuGet.config still decides: its <clear/> drops this file's sources and
        re-adds the same two.

      KEEP THE TWO FILES IN SYNC
        Same key ("local-feed") and same mapping patterns, so on the CLI the project config and this one merge
        into an identical mapping. A relative value resolves against the folder of the file that declares it,
        so both values name the same folder.

      WHAT FILLS THE FEED
        The parity project's _PackAllOptunaSharpPackagesIntoLocalFeed target, which packs every src/ project
        only while MSBuild itself restores (a CLI restore/build/test of that project or of the solution). An
        IDE restore consumes the feed as last packed; on a fresh clone, or after src/ changes, run
        `dotnet build test/OptunaSharp.Tests.PackageParity` once from a terminal (an IDE build of that project
        warns OSPP0001 when the feed is older than src/).
    -->
    <add key="local-feed" value="test/OptunaSharp.Tests.PackageParity/feed" />
  </packageSources>
  <!--
    The mapping is what keeps every restore reproducible with the extra source present: package ids
    starting with OptunaSharp resolve ONLY from the local feed (never a nuget.org shadow of the same
    version, the same pin the parity project's own config enforces); every other id resolves ONLY from
    nuget.org, exactly as before.

    Only the parity project references OptunaSharp* as packages; every other project uses ProjectReference.
    No other restore ever queries the local source, so a missing feed/ folder (a fresh clone) cannot fail them.
    FOOTGUN: a future project that wants a PUBLISHED OptunaSharp package from nuget.org must override this
    mapping in a NuGet.config of its own; under this file it would silently bind to the local feed.
  -->
  <packageSourceMapping>
    <packageSource key="nuget.org">
      <package pattern="*" />
    </packageSource>
    <packageSource key="local-feed">
      <package pattern="OptunaSharp*" />
    </packageSource>
  </packageSourceMapping>
</configuration>
